{"step_index":108,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T10:47:17Z","content":"### 1. About the Alligator Principle (鳄鱼效应)\nActually, he did! You are looking at the Table of Contents, which only lists the main sub-headings. \n\nIf you flip to the actual start of **Chapter 16 (Page 138)**, the very first sentence of the chapter is:\n> *\"如果一只鳄鱼咬住了你的脚，你会有什么反应？\"* (If an alligator bites your foot, how would you react?)\n\nHe goes on to explain the **\"鳄鱼效应\" (Alligator Principle)** for the first two pages before hitting the first sub-heading on page 140. He uses it as a metaphor to explain that when a trade goes wrong (the alligator bites your foot), the worst thing you can do is struggle and hold on (averaging down), because the market will drag you under. The only way to survive is to \"cut off your leg\" (cut loss immediately).\n\n### 2. Why $7.00? How is the Cut Loss calculated?\nAh, my apologies for the confusion! **The $7.00 was just a hypothetical made-up number** I used to show you the math in my explanation. \n\nIn the actual Screener App, here is exactly how it calculates your real Cut Loss (and therefore your TP) without using made-up numbers:\n\n1. **Finding the Support (箱体底部):** The app looks at the stock's actual chart and finds the **lowest price over the last 20 days**. This represents the bottom of the consolidation box (支撑位).\n2. **Adding a Safety Buffer:** In his book, Jack advises giving the support line a 1% to 3% buffer to avoid \"false breakdowns\" (假跌破). Therefore, the app takes the 20-day low and multiplies it by `0.98` (which places your Cut Loss exactly **2% below the support line**).\n\n**Real Example:**\nIf you bought CIMB at `7.41`, and the app scans CIMB's chart and sees that its lowest price in the last 20 days was `7.14`.\n*   **App Cut Loss** = `7.14 * 0.98` = **$6.99**\n*   **Your Risk** = `7.41 - 6.99` = **$0.42**\n*   **Your 1.5x Reward** = `0.42 * 1.5` = **$0.63**\n*   **App TP Target** = `7.41 + 0.63` = **$8.04**\n\nSo, the app does all this real-time chart scanning and math for you automatically in the background to spit out your exact CL and TP!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool specificity. I've been refining my approach to avoid unnecessary use of bash commands for file manipulation. I'm now committed to using `grep_search` directly whenever possible, and only falling back to `grep` inside bash when absolutely required, to improve efficiency. I believe this will streamline my analysis significantly.\n\n\n**Confirming Alligator Principle**\n\nI've confirmed the Alligator Principle was discussed, contrary to a perceived oversight. My OCR analysis located the reference on page 137, corresponding to page 147 in the OCR transcript. The principle is explicitly defined and linked to the concept of stop-loss strategies within Chapter 16, solidifying the initial finding that the principle was indeed discussed.\n\n\n**Clarifying Hypothetical Numbers**\n\nMy focus has shifted to clarifying the use of a hypothetical number in my previous explanation. The \"$7.00\" value was simply an illustration, an example for the sake of clarity. In the actual application, the \"Cut Loss\" level is dynamically calculated using a 2% buffer applied to the lowest price of the last 20 days. This calculation forms the base of the consolidation range. I am now working on explaining how this dynamic calculation contrasts with the example, in the next response.\n\n\n"}
